-- title:
--   Monod, p. 2 — G(ℤ) with rational breakpoints is exactly the set of its piecewise elements
-- statement:
--   A homeomorphism $f$ of the projective line $\mathbf P^1 = \mathbb R \cup \{\infty\}$ lies in `GRat`, the subgroup of `Gpp` generated by the homeomorphisms that are piecewise in $\mathrm{PSL}_2(\mathbb Z)$ with all breakpoints in $\mathbb Q \cup \{\infty\}$, exactly when $f$ itself is such a homeomorphism: $f \in$ `Gpp` and `IsPiecewiseProjOn ⊥ ratPoints f`. So the generated subgroup adds nothing: these elements already form a group.
--
--   `GRat`, `Gpp`, `IsPiecewiseProjOn` and `ratPoints` (the set $\mathbb Q \cup \{\infty\}$) are from the Monod definitions bundle, where `⊥` is the subring $\mathbb Z$ of $\mathbb R$.
--
--   Monod writes on p. 2: “The relation is as follows: if we modify the definition of $H(\mathbf{Z})$ by requiring that the breakpoints be rational, then all its elements are automatically $C^1$ and the resulting group is conjugated to $F$. The corresponding relation holds between $G(\mathbf{Z})$ and Thompson’s group $T$.” The bundle defines “$G(\mathbf{Z})$ with rational breakpoints” as a generated subgroup; this theorem says it is the set of rational-breakpoint elements, the group Monod's sentence speaks of, so that the published Thurston statements about `GRat` and `HRat` (conjugacy to Thompson's groups $T$ and $F$) are about Monod's groups. The proof follows the one for $G(A)$ (`Monod.mem_G_iff_isPiecewiseProj`): a Möbius map with integer entries sends $\mathbb Q \cup \{\infty\}$ to itself, so the breakpoints of a composite stay rational.
-- source:
--   Monod, N., Groups of piecewise projective homeomorphisms, Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. USA 110 (2013) 4524–4527, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1218426110 (arXiv:1209.5229v2, whose page numbers are used), p. 2

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_Monod_PiecewiseProjective

namespace Monod

theorem mem_GRat_iff (f : OnePoint ℝ ≃ₜ OnePoint ℝ) :
    f ∈ GRat ↔ f ∈ Gpp ∧ IsPiecewiseProjOn ⊥ ratPoints f := by
  sorry

end Monod
